<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The Scene Objects tab, against MoveIt's own Scene Objects tab
  (motion_planning_rviz_plugin_frame.ui, tab `scene_collision_objects`).

  MoveIt's tab is a QHBoxLayout of two QVBoxLayout columns:

    LEFT COLUMN
      Current Scene Objects      collision_objects_list   (QListWidget)
      Add/Remove scene object(s)  shape_size_x/y/z_spin_box
                                 shapes_combo_box
                                 add_object_button  [Add]  "at scene's origin"
                                 remove_object_button [Del] "Remove selected object"
                                 clear_scene_button [Clr]  "Clear planning scene"
    RIGHT COLUMN
      Change object pose/scale    object_x/y/z, object_rx/ry/rz, scene_scale   [NO EQUIVALENT]
      Object status               object_status  "Status"
      <spacer>
      Scene Geometry              &Publish  &Export  &Import                    [NO EQUIVALENT]

  Both right-column groups except "Object status" are ABSENT here, each for a
  named reason, and neither is stubbed with dead widgets:

  * "Change object pose/scale" — there is no service that edits an existing
    object. `add_object` creates one, `remove_object` deletes one, and nothing
    in between moves or rescales it. MoveIt can offer the group because its
    objects are values in a local PlanningScene that the widget owns, and it
    drives the change into the display and the planning scene itself; curobo's
    object store is the solver's, and the only handle on it is the name.
  * "Scene Geometry" — Publish / Export / Import are all
    PlanningSceneMonitor operations. curobo has no PlanningSceneMonitor, no
    .scene file format, and no service that accepts or returns a whole scene
    (`get_obstacles` returns newline-joined NAMES and nothing else — no
    geometry, no pose, no colour. `get_scene_objects` was added to fix
    exactly that and reports every field, but it is read-only: there is still
    no service that WRITES a pose or a scale back.)

  WHAT MOVED, AND WHY THE FORM IS LONGER THAN MOVEIT'S
  This group used to be a separate AddObjectsPanel widget stacked above the
  object list in a QSplitter, which is the single biggest reason the tab did not
  look like MoveIt's: MoveIt has one tab, two columns, and the add form in the
  left column. AddObjectsPanel's form is now IN this group, so the columns are
  MoveIt's columns and the form sits where MoveIt puts it.

  The form itself keeps every field it had, and adds MoveIt's shape combo plus
  the Add / Del / Clr toolbuttons, whose text and tooltips are MoveIt's own.
  curobo's AddObject.srv needs four things MoveIt does not ask for here,
  because MoveIt takes them from the interactive marker it puts in the 3D view:

    Name         MoveIt lets you rename a listed object inline (the list is
                 editable, and Ctrl+C duplicates). curobo's service matches on
                 name, and a name is the only key the object store has.
    Mesh path    required for MESH objects; AddObject.srv takes either this or
                 inline vertices/triangles, and this panel sends the path.
    Position     MoveIt adds at the origin and you drag it afterwards, which is
                 the group this tab cannot have.
    Orientation  Quaternion, four components — MoveIt's group spells rotation
                 the same way, in Euler XYZ, so only the frame differs.
    Color        std_msgs/ColorRGBA, four components.

  The "Position" / "Orientation" / "Color" ROW LABEL style is MoveIt's: its
  "Change object pose/scale" group writes "Position:" and "Rotation:" in the
  grid's first column and leaves the components unlabelled, so the components
  are named once per row rather than per field.

  Attach / Detach
  curobo has `attach_object` / `detach_object`, which MoveIt's version of this
  tab has no counterpart for (MoveIt attaches through the "Attached Collision
  Object" control on its Planning tab, and TrajectoryGoal has no such field).
  They are appended to MoveIt's own button row AFTER [Clr], so the four
  controls MoveIt puts there stay in the order MoveIt puts them in.
-->
